  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...SKC, decoupled winds, and increased moisture in\nthe onshore flow has led to the development of fog. Fog has \nbecome dense at times and a dense fog advisory has been issued \nfor zones where obs already show VIS<1/2mi like far SWern zones.\nThis fog is expected to expand to cover zones further N. Obs \nand current forecast reflect what the REFS and HREF data have \nbeen suggesting: a 50-70% chance for VIS < 1/4mi across most of \nENC outside of the immediate coast and far Nern tier of \ncounties. As a result, have a mix of patchy and areas of fog in \nthe grids across much of ENC through early morning hours.\n\nApproach of a trough aloft in the Ohio River Valley pushing E \nwill help shunt peaking upper ridging over the Mid- Atlantic\noffshore. High temps could flirt with record highs given the \nset up (SEE CLIMATE SECTION FOR MORE DETAILS). \n\nFor reference, normal highs in early October are around 80 degrees, \nand forecast highs are in the low 90s inland. Persistent \nsoutherly flow and increasing low- level moisture will lead to \nmuggier lows as well, well above Normal, mid to upper 60s FRI \nand SAT mornings.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...No real changes in forecast thinking this weekend \nas precipitation chances return as Gulf moisture opens up and a\ncold front drapes across the Sern Appalachians and pushes \nSEward slowly on SAT. As the next wave aloft approaches, the \nmoisture will squeeze into ENC, elevating rain chances for the \nweekend. While thunderstorms will be possible, the threat for \nsevere weather is very low as ML CAPES are quite low,\nunsurprising for early Oct.\n\nThe front loses steam as it approaches the coast and stalls, \nahead of a second reinforcing front spurred from the Great Lakes\nby a stronger trough aloft. For now, ensemble guidance has\nincreased to 60-75% chance of exceeding 1\" of storm total rain \nwith this system and the QPF grids reflect this with some HiRes\nblended in to show the higher accumulation potential. Any rain \nwill be helpful, especially for the coastal plain of ENC that is\ncurrently in moderate to extreme drought.\n\nWhere the front ends up SAT night and through the day SUN is\ndependent on the strength of the high to the N of the boundary,\nand where waves of weak low pressure pressure spin up. This will\ndictate what kind of liquid precip we'll get ahead of the next\nfront to cross MON. Should the high pressure spill over I95 \ncorridor, there's a chance that bulk of precip activity over \ninland zones will be more stratiform in nature with more\nconvective precip offshore and over the coast. Today's model \nsuites will feature more HiRes solutions now that we're getting\ninto the 72hr window.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Precip chances now diminish quickly late Sun\nnight and by Mon morning should be pushing off the OBX with \ndrying conditions expected early next week. Stiff NEerly breeze \n(peaking MON night into TUE) will lead to building seas and \nelevated water levels. Astronomical tide levels will luckily be \non the way down, but seas building to 7ft, though relatively\nchoppy periodicity of 7 sec, but enough to lead to further \nbeach and dune erosion concerns across the areas with severely \ndiminished dune structures. Strongest winds of 20-25 kt with \nhigher gusts expected MON night into TUE morning. Winds remain \nout of the N-NE into late- week, but speeds diminish to 10-15kt \nlate TUE and beyond.",
